mps are slated to return to the house of  commons today this is anti-vaccine  protesters converge on parliament hill  for a third straight day the  demonstrations brought the city to a  standstill over the weekend and  organizers say they're not leaving until  their demands are met so with more on  the situation and what's expected today  let's bring in our ottawa bureau chief  mercedes stevenson good morning mercedes  good morning how are you i am great  thanks for joining us this morning let's  talk about how things are shaping up in  ottawa this morning how might the  protest impact the parliamentary restart  well it's going to restart no matter  what the difficulty will be for some mps  who want to actually go to parliament  hill in person i went out before we sat  down to chat this morning and had a  little look around at what's happening  and i can tell you that what i last saw  was about seven blocks back from  parliament hill still pretty much  gridlocked on all of the main streets  going in there was one that had more  openings on it a street called elgin but  a lot of the ones that parliamentarians  use to go to work are blocked by trucks  that are either running or parked uh and  that has been the case since i first  started looking out uh early early this  morning while it was still dark now  there is a virtual parliament in session  so it won't actually stop any mps from  being able to do their jobs but they may  not be able to show up on parliament  hill and there's another question in  terms of security of how comfortable  people might feel trying to enter  parliament hill talking to some mps this  morning there was a warning from the  sergeant-at-arms he runs security late  last week that some members of this  protest group were saying they were  looking for mp's home addresses  so perhaps a few more will choose to  stay home i suspect several conservative  mps will still go this morning but it's  certainly whether you're an mp or just a  normal citizen here in ottawa has  completely snarled traffic for now the  fourth day in a row yeah there's really  no telling how long they plan to be  there they say they're not going to  leave until they  feel like they're being heard so what  are you hearing from protest organizers  and  what do they want exactly and do they  feel like  they're  making any  any  headway when it comes to being heard  well it doesn't appear that prime  minister justin trudeau has talked to  them aaron o'toole did prime minister  trudeau has actually moved to another  location over security concerns around  the protest coming in and i should say  that this has been a protest that has  had some extremist elements attached on  it was also very peaceful this weekend  while it was inconvenient um there  wasn't violence it was kind of like a  party atmosphere and people seemed to  just be blowing off steam after having  been inside with covid in terms of what  their demands are this seems to be  evolving and it's not consistent across  the group so it started with truckers  wanting the federal government to  reverse the vaccine mandate at the  border that is something that justin  trudeau does have the power to do  however he can't make joe biden change  the rules the president has the same  rule in place for truckers entering the  united states that then evolved into  getting rid of all vaccine mandates and  coveted restrictions many of which are  actually provincial governments and one  organizer patrick king who in the past  has been affiliated with far right  movements says that they're not leaving  until the liberal party is out of power  so as you can see this is a moving  target and it doesn't seem to be  consistent across the protesters with  what exactly they're looking for now in  terms of what happened over the weekend  we know there are some investigations  taking place what are you hearing from  police and  what does it look like when we're  talking about enforcement in the days to  come  i can tell you there's a lot of  frustration for people who live in  downtown ottawa because the noise has  been non-stop the honking is not just on  parliament hill it's for blocks and  blocks back  and it is going for hours and hours and  hours every day people can't drive into  or out of their homes they can't get  groceries  so there's certainly a sense of  frustration with the ottawa police by a  lot of residents who say they want them  to do more enforcement the police have  said they were prioritizing safety and  managing traffic they are now moving  into more of an enforcement posture it's  not clear really whether they're going  to forcibly clear protesters out at some  point uh the thought was sort of from  sources i was talking to let things go  over the weekend don't escalate things  maybe move in next week but with no sign  of this alleviating it's a big question  mark and it's costing a lot of money the  ottawa police are pegging this at eight  hundred thousand dollars a day now in  terms of what it is costing to police  this uh and of course as you saw there  was quite a bit of anger and outrage  over somebody dancing on the tomb of the  unknown soldier at the national war  memorial and the defacing of the terry  fox statue on parliament hill  most of the protest was peaceful but  some of those elements very disturbing  also seen confederate flags uh at least  two and a nazi flag as well so uh again  it's a very wide-ranging protest but  whatever the case it is still clogging  up the streets of ottawa and you're  gonna see more and more pressure i think  on the local government here in ottawa  to do something about that all right and  we know that you'll be keeping an eye on  that as well mercedes stevenson in  ottawa thanks so much
 

